  | The 'find' command is one of the most powerful         |
  | tools in the shell.                                    |
  |                                                        |
  | It searches for files and directories that match       |
  | a pattern you describe.                                |
  |                                                        |
  | Basic usage:                                           |
  |                                                        |
  | « find . -name 'pattern' »                             |
  |                                                        |
  | The dot means "start searching from here."             |
  | The -name flag matches file names.                     |
  |                                                        |
  | You can search for hidden files too:                   |
  |                                                        |
  | « find . -name '.*' »                                  |
  |                                                        |
  | To search the entire dungeon, return to the            |
  | entrance:                                              |
  |                                                        |
  | « find ~/bash-dungeon/Enter -name '*.fragment' »       |
  |                                                        |
